1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
In this section, we'll explain what file path traversal is, describe how to carry out path

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,100
traversal attacks and circumvent common obstacles, and spell out how to prevent path traversal

3
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:18,600
vulnerabilities.

4
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:28,100
File path traversal, also known as directory traversal, is a web security vulnerability

5
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:33,900
that allows an attacker to read arbitrary files on the server that is running an application.

6
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:38,660
This might include application code and data, credentials for back-end systems, and sensitive

7
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:40,800
operating system files.

8
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:46,200
In some cases, an attacker might be able to write to arbitrary files on the server, allowing

9
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:57,980
them to modify application data or behavior, and ultimately take full control of the server.

10
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:02,240
Consider a shopping application that displays images of items for sale.

11
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:08,100
Images are loaded via some HTML, like this.

12
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:15,580
The loadImageURL takes a file name parameter and returns the contents of the specified file.

13
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:20,280
The image files themselves are stored on a disk in this location.

14
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:25,400
To return an image, the application appends the requested file name to the base directory

15
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:30,220
and uses a file system API to read the contents of the file.

16
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:38,280
So for this request, the application reads from this file path.

17
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:43,100
The application implements no defenses against file path traversal attacks.

18
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:50,380
So an attacker can request this URL to retrieve an arbitrary file from the server's file systems.

19
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:54,740
This causes the application to read from this file path.

20
00:01:54,740 --> 00:02:00,860
The sequence dot dot slash is valid within a file path and means to step up one level in

21
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:02,880
the directory structure.

22
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:07,720
The three consecutive dot dot slash sequences step up to the file system root.

23
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:11,700
So this is the file that is actually read.

24
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:17,200
On Unix-based operating systems, the password file is a standard file containing details of

25
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:20,720
the users that are registered on the server.

26
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:25,540
On Windows, both forward slashes and backslashes are valid in file paths.

27
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:39,480
So an equivalent attack to retrieve a standard operating system file on Windows would be this.

28
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:44,520
Many applications that place user input into file paths implement some kind of defense against

29
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:49,180
path traversal attacks, and these can often be circumvented.

30
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:54,420
If an application strips or blocks directory traversal sequences from the user supplied file

31
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:57,960
name, then a range of bypasses might be possible.

32
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:03,040
You might be able to use an absolute path from the file system root to directly reference

33
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:06,920
a file without using any traversal sequences.

34
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:12,420
You might be able to use nested traversal sequences, which will revert to simple traversal sequences

35
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:15,300
when the inner sequence is stripped.

36
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:20,160
You might be able to use various non-standard encodings to bypass the input filter.

37
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:25,240
If an application requires that the user supplied file name must start with the expected base

38
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:30,740
folder, then it might be possible to include the required base folder followed by suitable

39
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:31,920
traversal sequences.

40
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:38,420
If an application requires that the user supplied file name must end with an expected file extension,

41
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:43,740
then it might be possible to use a null byte to effectively terminate the file path before

42
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:50,680
the required extension.

43
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:55,600
The most effective way to prevent file path traversal vulnerabilities is to avoid passing

44
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:59,980
user supplied input to file system APIs altogether.

45
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:04,520
Many application functions that do this can be rewritten to deliver the same behavior in

46
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:05,920
a safer way.

47
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:11,680
If it is considered unavoidable to pass user supplied input to the file system APIs, then

48
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:15,680
two layers of defense should be used together to prevent attacks.

49
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:20,680
Firstly, the application should validate the user input before processing it.

50
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:26,020
Ideally, the validation should compare against a whitelist of permitted values.

51
00:04:26,020 --> 00:04:31,180
If that isn't possible for the required functionality, then the validation should verify that the input

52
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:36,820
contains only permitted content, such as purely alphanumeric characters.

53
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:42,020
The second defense is that, after validating the supplied input, the application should append

54
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:48,700
the input to the base directory and use a platform file system API to conicalize the path.

55
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:54,280
It should verify that the conicalized path starts with the expected base directory.

